//
// Registry 221.1 #791 -- MINIMAL, DETERMINISTIC reproduction of the root.
//
// The row spent weeks described through its carrier: a polaris server whose
// /shutdown handler cancels its own governing token. Everything in that
// sentence turned out to be scenery. Measured 2026-09-01, the root is this:
//
//     a loop with no emitted safepoint makes a fiber at once
//     un-preemptible and un-cancellable.
//
// Both stopping paths need the fiber to arrive somewhere by itself. A stop_cb
// needs it to PARK -- this loop never parks. `cancel_requested` needs it to
// reach `nova_fiber_yield` -- and the only door to that is
// `nova_preempt_check`, which the codegen emits in function PROLOGUES, not on
// loop back-edges. A loop with no calls inside it therefore contains no
// safepoint at all. `detach` completes the picture: the orphan scope has no
// owner to notice, and the atexit orphan drain waits on its pending_remote
// forever.
//
// Judged by process termination, never by stdout. Build and run:
//
//   nova-cli/target/release/nova.exe build docs/plans/repro/p791/spin.nv -o /tmp/spin.exe
//   timeout 20 /tmp/spin.exe; echo "exit=$?"
//
// exit=124 means the hang reproduced (killed by the timeout). exit=0 means it
// is fixed. Measured on 2026-09-01: exit=124, every time, with the watchdog
// dump showing `pre-exit-drain-10s-remote-1` and one fiber at
// `mco_status=2` (that is MCO_RUNNING -- see minicoro.h:263, the numbering
// that cost this row three false trails), `fstate=1`, `hdl=0`, `stop_cb=0`,
// and a STATIC `parent_scope` equal to the global orphan scope.
//
// This file is the class-level fixture acceptance point (3) of the row asks
// for: it has no network, no cancellation and no timing in it, so it cannot
// flake, and it will go green only when the root is actually fixed.

module main

import std.io.{println}

fn main() Io Detach -> () {
    detach {
        mut i int = 0
        while true {
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    println("main done")
}
